## Goal
Perform principal component analysis (PCA) on the normalized gene counts to visualize sample relationships. Generate a 2D PCA plot where each sample is colored by condition (treated vs. untreated) to assess the treatment effect and sample clustering patterns.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. Load the gene count matrix (counts_gene) and design table using Python 3 in gars-bio environment.
2. Filter genes: retain those with mean CPM ≥ 1 across all samples to remove low-abundance noise.
3. Normalize counts using TMM (trimmed mean of M-values) via edgeR-equivalent approach in Python (scipy).
4. Log-transform normalized counts as log2(CPM + 1).
5. Perform PCA using scikit-learn's StandardScaler and decomposition.PCA on the log-transformed counts, retaining components explaining 50% of variance (typically PC1 and PC2).
6. Create a scatter plot (matplotlib) showing PC1 vs PC2, with points colored by condition (untreated: blue, treated: red).
7. Add axis labels showing percent variance explained, legend, and title.
8. Save plot as `results/sample_pca.png` (300 dpi).
9. Save PCA coordinates and variance components to `results/pca_coords.csv` for reference.

All steps run on login node (computationally lightweight: 6 samples, 22,783 genes; <1 second execution).

